{
  "slug": "maximal-rectangle",
  "title": "Largest Solid Banner",
  "topic": "stack",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "pattern": "monotonic stack / row-wise histogram DP",
  "statement": "A venue's seating chart is given as `grid`, a rectangular matrix of integers where `grid[i][j]` is `1` for a usable cell and `0` for a blocked one. A banner is an axis-aligned rectangle: it covers a contiguous range of rows and a contiguous range of columns, and every cell underneath it must be usable. Return the area of the largest banner that fits, counted as the number of cells it covers. If no cell is usable, return 0.\n\nFor `grid = [[1, 0, 1], [1, 1, 1], [1, 0, 1]]` the answer is 3: the middle row and the first column are each a usable run of three cells, and no rectangle of four cells is fully usable. For `grid = [[1, 1], [1, 1]]` the answer is 4, and for `grid = [[0]]` the answer is 0.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= grid.length <= 30`, every row of `grid` has the same length, and `0 <= grid[i] <= 1` for every row `i`, so every cell of the matrix is either `0` or `1`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "grid = [[0]]",
      "output": "0",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "grid = [[1]]",
      "output": "1",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(grid: number[][])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Try every rectangle and test it. With at most 30 rows and 30 columns all four boundaries (top, bottom, left, right) can be enumerated, and a 2D prefix sum of usable cells answers 'how many `1` cells lie inside this rectangle?' in O(1).",
    "Look at the grid one row at a time and keep a per-column height: the number of usable cells ending at the current row. Every banner whose bottom edge lies on this row becomes a rectangle in that 1D array of heights, which the largest-rectangle-in-a-histogram routine solves.",
    "Keep a stack of column indices whose heights increase. When a shorter height arrives, pop and settle each bar: the arriving column is its right edge and the bar now on top of the stack is its left edge. Push the arriving column, run the loop once more with a height of 0 to flush the stack, and take the best area seen."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force precomputes a 2D prefix sum of usable cells, then enumerates all four banner boundaries (top row, bottom row, left column, right column). A candidate rectangle is usable exactly when the number of `1` cells inside it equals its area, which the prefix sum answers in O(1), so the whole scan costs O(R\u00b2 C\u00b2). The optimal presses the same idea into one dimension: scanning rows top to bottom it keeps `heights[c]`, the length of the run of usable cells ending at the current row in column `c`. Every banner whose bottom edge lies on the current row is a rectangle in that histogram of heights, so the row's contribution comes from the largest-rectangle-in-a-histogram routine driven by a monotonic stack. When a shorter bar arrives, the popped bar's area is `heights[k] * (currentColumn - stackTop - 1)`, where `stackTop` is the bar left on top of the stack after the pop.",
    "why_optimal": "The stack scan touches each cell a constant number of times: one height update per cell, one push per column per row, and at most one pop per push. O(R C) is therefore optimal, since every cell must be read at least once to learn whether it is usable, and the auxiliary state is only the C heights plus the stack. The brute force is affordable only because the bounds cap the grid at 30 x 30, where it runs about 10^5 rectangle tests; the same loops on a 200 x 200 grid would need roughly 4 x 10^8 tests and time out.",
    "pitfalls": "Popping with `>` instead of `>=` is the commonest bug: bars of equal height must be popped too, otherwise a rectangle is settled against a bar of the same height and its width is over-counted. Read the popped height before calling `pop()`, because the element is gone afterwards. The stack has to be flushed after the last column, which is why the loop runs one step past the end with a virtual height of 0. A cell counts as usable only when it is exactly `1`, so compare against the integer and not against a truthy value. Boundary inputs decide whether the code is right: a single `[[0]]` must return 0 while a single `[[1]]` must return 1, and a grid whose rows are empty must not throw."
  },
